steps.say:
StoneGolem and ClayGolem both extend Golem; only StoneGolem has smash().
Golem g = new ClayGolem();
StoneGolem s = (StoneGolem) g;
s.smash();
The cast is my promise to the compiler that g holds a StoneGolem. It compiles. But at run time the object is clay, the promise is false, and the program dies with a ClassCastException.

beats.code:
Golem g = new ClayGolem();
StoneGolem s = (StoneGolem) g;
s.smash();

steps.say:
So ask first. The instanceof operator tests an object's runtime type, and modern Java lets you name a new variable right inside the test.
if (g instanceof StoneGolem s) {
    s.smash();
}
If g holds a StoneGolem, then s is that same object, already typed as StoneGolem - no cast to write. This form is called a type pattern.

